Cell["\<\
Here is the twist. A classical light source, if it is allowed to report \
continuous brightness fractions instead of discrete particle clicks, can be \
tuned so that it produces exactly the same numbers as the real quantum device \
\[LongDash] not approximately, but to the last digit. Tuned to transmission t \
= 1/Sqrt[5], the intensity emulator reproduces the quantum table cell for \
cell, so at that one tuning the two devices' input-output statistics are \
identical and no amount of further statistics can separate them. This is a \
theorem, not a failure of effort, and it is an established experimental fact \
that suitably tuned classical fields reach Sqrt[5] on scenarios of exactly \
this kind [10, 11].\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2b161c7d-9e58-1e40-846c-f0587d9b44e3"],

Cell["\<\
Two different physical devices, one identical output \[LongDash] and why no \
test on the numbers can separate them:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"5847f1d3-07a4-7b4f-8237-abe6a4f77af3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{},
 Graphics[{
   {RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Disk[{-3.5, 1}, 0.24]}, White, Text[Style[\"\
\[Gamma]\", 12], {-3.5, 1}],
   Black, Text[Style[\"quantum photon\", 8], {-3.5, 0.5}],
   {RGBColor[.85, .55, .2], Thickness[0.02], Line[{{-3.75, -0.8}, {-3.25, \
-1.2}}], Line[{{-3.75, -1.2}, {-3.25, -0.8}}]},
   Black, Text[Style[\"classical optics\", 8], {-3.5, -1.5}],
   Arrowheads[0.028], GrayLevel[0.4],
   Arrow[{{-3.0, 0.9}, {-1.25, 0.25}}], Arrow[{{-3.0, -0.9}, {-1.25, \
-0.25}}],
   {GrayLevel[0.96], EdgeForm[GrayLevel[0.5]], Rectangle[{-1.15, -0.62}, \
{1.35, 0.62}]},
   Black, Text[Style[\"one identical output table\", 9, Bold], {0.1, 0.35}],
   Text[Style[\"S = \[Sqrt]5,   CF = 2\[Sqrt]5-4 = 0.472\", 9], {0.1, 0.02}],
   RGBColor[.6, .3, .1], Text[Style[\"every C1\[Dash]C5 gate: PASS\", 8], \
{0.1, -0.34}],
   GrayLevel[0.4], Arrow[{{1.45, 0}, {2.4, 0}}],
   RGBColor[.8, .25, .15], Text[Style[\"\[Times]\", 26], {3.05, 0.12}],
   Black, Text[Style[\"no statistical test\", 8], {3.95, 0.08}], \
Text[Style[\"can tell them apart\", 8], {3.95, -0.15}],
   RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Text[Style[\"the blind spot: identical statistics, \
different physics \[LongDash] only the geometric lens (Stage 5) separates \
them\", 9, Bold], {-0.35, -2.05}]
   }, ImageSize -> 600, PlotRange -> {{-4.4, 5.2}, {-2.4, 1.6}}, ImagePadding \
-> 6]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[746]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6560c66d-fafd-9445-a511-750135ed718c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 GraphicsBox[{
   {RGBColor[0.2, 0.45, 0.85], DiskBox[{-3.5, 1}, 0.24]}, 
   {GrayLevel[1], InsetBox[
     StyleBox["\<\"\[Gamma]\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontSize->12], {-3.5, 1}]}, 
   {GrayLevel[0], InsetBox[
     StyleBox["\<\"quantum photon\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontSize->8], {-3.5, 0.5}], 
    {RGBColor[0.85, 0.55, 0.2], Thickness[0.02], 
     LineBox[{{-3.75, -0.8}, {-3.25, -1.2}}], 
     LineBox[{{-3.75, -1.2}, {-3.25, -0.8}}]}}, 
   {GrayLevel[0], InsetBox[
     StyleBox["\<\"classical optics\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontSize->8], {-3.5, -1.5}]}, 
   {GrayLevel[0.4], Arrowheads[0.028], ArrowBox[{{-3., 0.9}, {-1.25, 0.25}}], 
    ArrowBox[{{-3., -0.9}, {-1.25, -0.25}}], 
    {GrayLevel[0.96], EdgeForm[GrayLevel[0.5]], 
     RectangleBox[{-1.15, -0.62}, {1.35, 0.62}]}, 
    {GrayLevel[0], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"one identical output table\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->9,
       FontWeight->Bold], {0.1, 0.35}], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"S = \[Sqrt]5,   CF = 2\[Sqrt]5-4 = 0.472\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->9], {0.1, 0.02}]}, 
    {RGBColor[0.6, 0.3, 0.1], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"every C1\[Dash]C5 gate: PASS\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->8], {0.1, -0.34}]}, 
    {GrayLevel[0.4], ArrowBox[{{1.45, 0}, {2.4, 0}}]}, 
    {RGBColor[0.8, 0.25, 0.15], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"\[Times]\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->26], {3.05, 0.12}]}, 
    {GrayLevel[0], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"no statistical test\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->8], {3.95, 0.08}], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"can tell them apart\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->8], {3.95, -0.15}]}, 
    {RGBColor[0.2, 0.45, 0.85], InsetBox[
      StyleBox["\<\"the blind spot: identical statistics, different physics \
\[LongDash] only the geometric lens (Stage 5) separates them\"\>",
       StripOnInput->False,
       FontSize->9,
       FontWeight->Bold], {-0.35, -2.05}]}}},
  ImagePadding->6,
  ImageSize->600,
  PlotRange->{{-4.4, 5.2}, {-2.4, 1.6}}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.993047705942932*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[746]=",ExpressionUUID->"73c83552-965a-f048-8e0b-8920497d4ecf"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Both devices produce exactly the same output table, both node sums equal \
Sqrt[5], and both contextual fractions equal 2 Sqrt[5] - 4. Every Category-1 \
gate passes \[LongDash] which means the emulator earns a QUANTUM-CERTIFIED \
verdict it does not deserve. This is the framework's one designed-in blind \
spot, and it is the whole reason a second, completely different kind of \
evidence is needed. Statistics have run out; geometry has not.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"733f8698-8cca-de48-9c82-eadae6bfc08e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Stage 5 \[LongDash] The Geometric Lens (Proposition 2): How Twisted Is the \
Motion?\
\>", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"ef62edf1-a73c-a84d-8cb4-2268e982a790"],

